TILELOADD* / TILESTORED: no-SIB forms executed, and a SIB byte with no index used RSP as the stride

The SDM requires SIB addressing for TILELOADD, TILELOADDT1, TILELOADDRS,
TILELOADDRST1 and TILESTORED, and says "if no index register in the SIB
encoding, the value zero is used" for the stride. The handlers tried to
raise #UD with

  if (i->sibIndex() == BX_NIL_REGISTER)

but the 64-bit decoder records both a missing SIB byte and a SIB byte whose
index field is 100b (without VEX.X) as index 4, which is RSP's number;
BX_NIL_REGISTER is 19. So the check never fired, and in both cases

  stride = BX_READ_64BIT_REG(i->sibIndex()) << i->sibScale()

read RSP: `tileloadd tmm0, [rdi]` and `[rip+disp32]` executed instead of
raising #UD, and `tileloadd tmm0, [rdi+riz]` loaded rows RSP bytes apart
instead of repeating the first one (the same for tilestored).

Past the decoder the two cases cannot be told apart, so the SIB check moves
to decoder_vex64: a memory form of these five opcodes with ModRM.rm != 100b
decodes as BX_IA_ERROR (#UD). The handlers drop the dead check and use a
stride of 0 when the index is 4; index 12 (r12, VEX.X set) is unaffected.

Fixed in decoder/fetchdecode64.cc and avx/amx.cc. Found 2026-09-26 by
diffing the Bochs decoder against binutils 2.45 over the VEX/EVEX encoding
space: binutils marked the no-SIB forms (bad), Bochs decoded them.

